//? Title: Gradient Clipping and Accumulation: Geometry and Weighting
//? Subtitle: Average sample gradients, clip the result, then apply one optimizer update
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Large gradients can destabilize an update, while memory limits may require a logical
batch to be processed as several smaller batches. We need to specify both the clipping
geometry and the weights used when recombining microbatch means. Exact two-coordinate
vectors expose value versus norm clipping; an unequal partition of seven observations
checks accumulation against a full-batch derivative. A cancellation example proves that
clipping before averaging gives a different answer. These controlled constructions do
not estimate a model's accuracy or imply a bound on every adaptive optimizer's step.
)
\toc

> The method


Global norm clipping concatenates all parameter gradients conceptually and applies one
shared scale. At threshold C>0,

$$
 \widetilde g=g\min(1,C/\|g\|_2).
$$
\alttext(Global norm clipping rescales the whole gradient vector only when its norm exceeds the threshold.)

This is the Euclidean projection onto the radius-C ball. It preserves direction for a
nonzero vector and leaves zero unchanged. Clipping each tensor separately defines a
different constraint; value clipping constrains each coordinate and can change direction.
For plain SGD the update norm is at most eta*C after global clipping. Momentum memory
and adaptive rescaling mean that bound does not automatically apply to their updates.

If microbatch j contains n_j samples and g_j is the gradient of its mean loss, the
logical full-batch mean gradient at fixed parameters is

$$
 g=\frac{\sum_j n_j g_j}{\sum_j n_j}.
$$
\alttext(Microbatch mean gradients must be weighted by their sample counts before division by the total count.)

Use GradientAccumulator.add(g_j,n_j), take mean(), clip once if desired, and call step()
once. No optimizer update or parameter-dependent state change may intervene if equivalence
to a full-batch derivative is intended. Nonseparable losses, batch normalization and
random layers can also make microbatch and full-batch forwards different. Accumulation
does not reproduce those forwards merely by choosing correct weights. Reset the
accumulator after applying an update. An empty mean is an error, not a zero-gradient step.


The concept diagram is generated from the retained local TikZ source. LaTeX with
PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm, is needed only to regenerate it.

