> Simple examples

>> A vector with norm five

For g=(3,4), a global norm limit two gives (1.2,1.6), with norm two and unchanged
direction. Value clipping to [−2,2] gives (2,2), with norm sqrt(8)>2 and a different
direction. Supplying the two coordinates as separate scalar tensors still uses the same
global norm when calling clip_by_global_norm().

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/clipping_geometry.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto g     = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{3, 4});
  const auto norm  = dm::nn::clip_by_global_norm({g}, 2);
  const auto value = dm::nn::clip_by_value({g}, -2, 2);
  const auto a = norm.first[0].to_vector<double>(), b = value[0].to_vector<double>();
  if (std::abs(a[0] - 1.2) > 1e-14 || std::abs(a[1] - 1.6) > 1e-14)
    throw std::runtime_error("norm clipping mismatch");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("rule", std::vector<std::string>{"original", "global norm", "by value"});
  t.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{3, a[0], b[0]});
  t.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{4, a[1], b[1]});
  t.add_column(
      "norm",
      std::vector<double>{
          norm.second, dm::nn::global_norm(norm.first), dm::nn::global_norm(value)});
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines({0, 3}, {0, 4}, "original", {31, 41, 55})
      .lines({0, a[0]}, {0, a[1]}, "global norm", {37, 99, 235})
      .lines({0, b[0]}, {0, b[1]}, "by value", {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Norm and coordinate constraints differ")
      .x_label("gradient coordinate one")
      .y_label("gradient coordinate two")
      .x_limits(-.2, 4)
      .y_limits(-.2, 6);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(850, 480).save_svg("build/clipping_geometry.svg");
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| rule | x | y | norm |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| original | 3.0 | 4.0 | 5.000000 |
| global norm | 1.2 | 1.6 | 2.000000 |
| by value | 2.0 | 2.0 | 2.828427 |

\image(figures/clipping_geometry.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Global clipping constrains the vector norm; value clipping constrains a box and may rotate the gradient.)
\alttext(Three vectors from the origin show the original gradient, its norm-clipped version and its value-clipped version. A table compares coordinates and Euclidean norms.)

>> Unequal microbatches need sample weights

At slope three and intercept minus one, use y=2x+1 on the seven integer inputs
−3 through three. The mean squared-error gradient is exactly (8,−4). Partition
into sizes 2,3,2 while keeping the parameters fixed. The unweighted average gives the
three-example middle batch the same weight as a two-example outer batch; the slope
gradient therefore differs from eight. This example uses a separable deterministic loss.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/clipping_accumulation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  auto w       = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{3}).requires_grad_(),
       b       = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-1}).requires_grad_();
  const auto x = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3}),
             y = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-5, -3, -1, 1, 3, 5, 7});
  dm::nn::GradientAccumulator weighted, unweighted;
  std::int64_t                start = 0;
  for (int size : {2, 3, 2}) {
    std::vector<std::int64_t> ids;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i)
      ids.push_back(start++);
    const auto bx = dm::nn::take_rows(x, ids), by = dm::nn::take_rows(y, ids);
    const auto g = dm::nn::grad(dm::nn::mean(dm::nn::square(bx * w + b - by)), {w, b});
    weighted.add(g, size);
    unweighted.add(g);
  }
  const auto full = dm::nn::grad(dm::nn::mean(dm::nn::square(x * w + b - y)), {w, b}),
             a = weighted.mean(), c = unweighted.mean();
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
    if (std::abs(a[j].item<double>() - full[j].item<double>()) > 1e-13)
      throw std::runtime_error("weighted accumulation differs");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column(
      "method", std::vector<std::string>{"full batch", "sample weighted", "unweighted batches"});
  t.add_column(
      "slope_gradient",
      std::vector<double>{full[0].item<double>(), a[0].item<double>(), c[0].item<double>()});
  t.add_column(
      "bias_gradient",
      std::vector<double>{full[1].item<double>(), a[1].item<double>(), c[1].item<double>()});
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars(
       {0, 1, 2}, t.numeric_column("slope_gradient").values(), "slope gradient", {37, 99, 235}, .5)
      .abline_h(8)
      .title("Unequal partitions require sample weights")
      .x_label("0 = full, 1 = weighted, 2 = unweighted")
      .y_label("slope derivative")
      .y_limits(0, 12);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(900, 430).save_svg("build/clipping_accumulation.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\nAccumulated sample weight: {:.0f}.\n", t.to_md(9), weighted.total_weight());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | slope_gradient | bias_gradient |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| full batch | 8.00000000 | -4 |
| sample weighted | 8.00000000 | -4 |
| unweighted batches | 9.11111111 | -4 |


Accumulated sample weight: 7.
\image(figures/clipping_accumulation.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The weighted microbatch mean matches a direct full-batch derivative at fixed parameters.)
\alttext(Three bars compare full-batch, sample-weighted and unweighted slope gradients. The reference line is eight; a table also gives the bias gradients and total sample weight.)

> Checking the theory

>> Clipping does not commute with averaging

Take two equally weighted gradients (3,0) and (−1,0), with norm threshold one.
Their mean is (1,0), already on the boundary. Clipping that mean leaves it unchanged.
Clipping each microbatch first gives (1,0) and (−1,0), whose average is zero. Thus
these pipelines can produce different updates even at identical weights. Clipping each
microbatch may be intentional for other objectives, but it does not reproduce the
clipped full-batch gradient. In particular, per-example clipping in privacy methods has
a different purpose and must not be replaced by the aggregate operation here.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/clipping_order.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto a = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{3, 0}),
             b = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-1, 0});
  dm::nn::GradientAccumulator raw, clipped;
  raw.add({a});
  raw.add({b});
  clipped.add(dm::nn::clip_by_global_norm({a}, 1).first);
  clipped.add(dm::nn::clip_by_global_norm({b}, 1).first);
  const auto mean = raw.mean(), after = dm::nn::clip_by_global_norm(mean, 1).first,
             before = clipped.mean();
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column(
      "pipeline", std::vector<std::string>{"mean gradient", "mean then clip", "clip then mean"});
  t.add_column(
      "first_coordinate",
      std::vector<double>{
          mean[0].to_vector<double>()[0],
          after[0].to_vector<double>()[0],
          before[0].to_vector<double>()[0]});
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars(
       {0, 1, 2},
       t.numeric_column("first_coordinate").values(),
       "first coordinate",
       {37, 99, 235},
       .5)
      .title("The order of nonlinear operations matters")
      .x_label("0 = mean, 1 = mean then clip, 2 = clip then mean")
      .y_label("update direction coordinate")
      .y_limits(0, 1.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(950, 430).save_svg("build/clipping_order.svg");
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| pipeline | first_coordinate |
| :--- | ---: |
| mean gradient | 1 |
| mean then clip | 1 |
| clip then mean | 0 |

\image(figures/clipping_order.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Nonlinearity makes the order of aggregation and clipping observable even in a two-gradient construction.)
\alttext(A table and bars show first-coordinate values one, one and zero for the three pipelines.)

> Conclusion

Clipping specifies a constraint geometry, and accumulation specifies the weighting of
mean gradients at a fixed model state. Use one well-defined logical update, preserve
sample weights for uneven batches, and choose clipping order deliberately. These examples
validate identities and a counterexample, rather than promising a universal training or
privacy guarantee.

> References

- Pascanu, R., Mikolov, T. and Bengio, Y. (2013). [On the difficulty of training Recurrent Neural Networks|https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.5063]. Gradient norm clipping and exploding gradients.
- TensorFlow authors. [clip_by_global_norm|https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/clip_by_global_norm]. Shared-norm clipping semantics.
